[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one parser bug, one fix queued. Read the reports
rather than their subjects or intake tags, and #4471 holds three problems. The queued fix
closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Fixing the parser leaves the other two problems open.
</Callout>

## What the reports show

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

- **`parser`**: a day-first tenant's date comes back with day and month swapped ("8 January"
  shows as "1 August"). The shared parser reads every date month first. This is the bug the
  queued fix is for.
- **`hint`**: the same swap, but only through the booking form of booking-v3 tenants. That
  form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field, so people type month first, and the form
  reads it day first. Fixing the parser doesn't touch it. The fix is the hint.
- **`oneDayMove`**: the date moved by one day ("Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday",
  "Lisbon got Tuesday, Toronto got Monday", "only June is one day off"). A parser that reads
  month first can only swap day and month; it can't move a date by one day. This looks like
  a time zone or daylight-saving shift. The desk had already noted these reports
  (`desk-notes.json`), and #4471's cause table still counted them under `parser`.

## How they were counted

- The export comes in overlapping pages, so one report can appear twice. Each report id
  counts once.
- The cause comes from the tenant (locale and booking form) and the channel, as in
  `causes.ts`. The intake tag doesn't decide it: it is a guess from the reporter's browser.
- Subjects don't decide it either. The same subject ("Date is wrong") covers more than one
  problem, which is why merging on subject ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject))
  put them all in one ticket.

## Lessons

- Read what a report shows, not what it is called. A big ticket can hide more than one bug.
- A fix is only "queued" for the problem it fixes.

